      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Director Edmund Goulding, who previously directed the dramatic heavyweight <a href=\"/movie/27972\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"27972\">Dark Victory</a> (1939), brings a light touch to what could have been scandalous material for the time. The episodic structure - showing how different couples react to their \"unmarriage\" - prefigures later anthology films like <a href=\"/movie/42514\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"42514\">Plaza Suite</a> (1971) and allows for a range of tones from broad farce to gentle romance. Particularly notable is the young Marilyn Monroe's segment as a beauty contest winner, showing her comedic chops just before her breakthrough in <a href=\"/movie/759\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"759\">Gentlemen Prefer Blondes</a> (1953)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's exploration of marriage as both a legal and emotional contract echoes through later classics like <a href=\"/movie/492188\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"492188\">Marriage Story</a> (2019) and <a href=\"/movie/661\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"661\">The Marriage of Maria Braun</a> (1979). Its structure of interconnected relationship stories influenced works like <a href=\"/movie/508\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"508\">Love Actually</a> (2003) and <a href=\"/movie/32856\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"32856\">Valentine's Day</a> (2010), though those films lack the sharp social commentary beneath the comedy. SUBHEAD: Marriage in 1950s Cinema"
      },
